  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Mahle gave them five innings on 10 hits and they couldn't push across more than two runs to back it.",
    "what_this_means": "The game tightened after Oakland's four-run fifth, but the Giants never found the at-bat when it mattered. Arraez went 4-for-5 with a homer. The kind of night a lineup dreams about. Yet he was the only one doing real damage at the plate. The bullpen did its part, three arms combining for three scoreless innings after the starter left. Hentges, Borucki, and Beck closed the door. But innings six and seven arrived with runners in scoring position and nobody converted either one. That's where the night broke. Seven strikeouts mixed into ten hits meant the offense was there in form but not in finish. The scoreboard said 2\u20135, but it could have said anything once the second inning arrived and Oakland broke through. A loss like this isn't about talent. It's about the margin between a moment showing up and a moment mattering.",
    "what_to_watch": "Seven runners left on base. Nothing to show for them.",
